#: ../../../explanation/how-snaps-work/channels-and-tracks.md:0
msgid ""
"Understand snap channels and tracks, including their purpose, risk levels, "
"branches, channel closure, and release selection behavior."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../explanation/how-snaps-work/channels-and-tracks.md:8
msgid "Channels and tracks"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../explanation/how-snaps-work/channels-and-tracks.md:10
msgid ""
"Channels are an important snap concept. They define which release of a snap "
"is installed and tracked for updates."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../explanation/how-snaps-work/channels-and-tracks.md:12
msgid ""
"The complete channel name can be structured as three distinct parts "
"separated by slashes:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../explanation/how-snaps-work/channels-and-tracks.md:14
msgid "<track>/<risk>/<branch>\n"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../explanation/how-snaps-work/channels-and-tracks.md:18
msgid ""
"The _tracking_ value for an installed snap, as shown in the output from the "
"`snap list` command, shows which channel is installed and is being followed "
"for that snap."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../explanation/how-snaps-work/channels-and-tracks.md:20
msgid ""
"To view the channel a snap is tracking, the following command may also be "
"used:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../explanation/how-snaps-work/channels-and-tracks.md:22
msgid "snap refresh --tracking vlc\n"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../explanation/how-snaps-work/channels-and-tracks.md:26
msgid "Tracks"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../explanation/how-snaps-work/channels-and-tracks.md:28
msgid ""
"All snaps have a default track. Without the snap developer specifying "
"otherwise, the default track is called *latest*. Similarly, when no track is "
"specified, a snap will implicitly install from the *latest* track. The track "
"can also be specified explicitly:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../explanation/how-snaps-work/channels-and-tracks.md:30
msgid "snap install vlc --channel=latest/edge\n"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../explanation/how-snaps-work/channels-and-tracks.md:34
msgid ""
"Developers can optionally choose whether to supplement the default _latest_ "
"track with additional tracks. The developer is also free to designate one of "
"these as the default track to be installed when no further preference is "
"given."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../explanation/how-snaps-work/channels-and-tracks.md:36
msgid ""
"Mozilla's [Firefox snap](https://snapcraft.io/firefox) is a good example of "
"how tracks can be used. It contains two tracks: the default _latest_ track "
"for the majority of users, and an  _esr_ track for the version of Firefox "
"with [extended support](https://www.firefox.com/en-GB/browsers/enterprise/)."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../explanation/how-snaps-work/channels-and-tracks.md:38
msgid ""
"Equally, a track could also be used to track minor updates (2.0.1, 2.0.2), "
"major updates (2.1, 2.2), or releases held for long-term support (3.2, 4.1)."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../explanation/how-snaps-work/channels-and-tracks.md:40
msgid ""
"Tracks are listed in the *channels* section of the output from the `snap "
"info` command:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../explanation/how-snaps-work/channels-and-tracks.md:42
msgid ""
"$ snap info firefox\n"
"[...]\n"
"channels:\n"
"  latest/stable:    149.0.2-1     2026-04-08 (8107) 287MB -\n"
"  latest/candidate: 150.0-1       2026-04-16 (8172) 288MB -\n"
"  latest/beta:      150.0b10-1    2026-04-15 (8166) 288MB -\n"
"  latest/edge:      151.0a1       2026-04-17 (8175) 310MB -\n"
"  esr/stable:       140.9.1esr-1  2026-04-07 (8106) 259MB -\n"
"  esr/candidate:    140.10.0esr-1 2026-04-15 (8162) 259MB -\n"
"  esr/beta:         ↑                                     \n"
"  esr/edge:         ↑                                     \n"
"[...]\n"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../explanation/how-snaps-work/channels-and-tracks.md:57
msgid ""
"In the above output, the Firefox snap includes the custom *esr* track. You "
"can also see which tracks a snap supports by clicking *Other versions* from "
"its online Store entry."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../explanation/how-snaps-work/channels-and-tracks.md:59
msgid ""
"To install Firefox from its *insider* track, for example, use the following "
"command:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../explanation/how-snaps-work/channels-and-tracks.md:61
msgid "snap install firefox --channel=esr/stable\n"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../explanation/how-snaps-work/channels-and-tracks.md:65
msgid ""
"If you already have Firefox installed, you can switch channel with the `snap "
"refresh` command:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../explanation/how-snaps-work/channels-and-tracks.md:67
msgid "snap refresh firefox --channel=esr/stable\n"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../explanation/how-snaps-work/channels-and-tracks.md:71
msgid ""
"Developers must currently make a request for tracks to be added to their "
"snap via the [#store-requests](https://forum.snapcraft.io/c/store-requests) "
"forum category. Releases are verified and checked to ensure that reasonable "
"user expectations are being  met. For example, only _3.2.\\*_ versions are "
"accepted into a _3.2_ track."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../explanation/how-snaps-work/channels-and-tracks.md:73
msgid "Risk-levels"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../explanation/how-snaps-work/channels-and-tracks.md:75
msgid ""
"There are four risk-levels: stable, candidate, beta and edge. These "
"represent decreasing levels of stability for a snap, although that decision "
"is ultimately up to the snap's publisher."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../explanation/how-snaps-work/channels-and-tracks.md:77
msgid ""
"Installing from a less stable risk-level will typically mean that updates "
"for a snap will be more frequent than for a more stable risk-level."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../explanation/how-snaps-work/channels-and-tracks.md:79
msgid "The risk-levels have the following meaning:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../explanation/how-snaps-work/channels-and-tracks.md:81
msgid ""
"**stable**:  for the vast majority of users running on production "
"environments."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../explanation/how-snaps-work/channels-and-tracks.md:83
msgid ""
"Releases at this risk level are as stable as they will ever get, according "
"to the project's standards. Important software will only reach this stage "
"once it’s ready for production use and may be used in products. There is an "
"implied promise to avoid any changes that would disrupt those usages."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../explanation/how-snaps-work/channels-and-tracks.md:85
msgid ""
"**candidate**: for users who need to test updates prior to stable "
"deployment, or those verifying whether a specific issue has been resolved."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../explanation/how-snaps-work/channels-and-tracks.md:87
msgid ""
"Candidate releases are considered almost ready for going into stable, "
"but     need some additional real world experimentation before they move "
"forward. Software reaching this stage will typically have passed all "
"available QA and review processes, since users following it expect a high "
"stability level. Should almost never break."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../explanation/how-snaps-work/channels-and-tracks.md:89
msgid ""
"**beta**: for users wanting to test the latest features, typically outside "
"of a production environment."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../explanation/how-snaps-work/channels-and-tracks.md:91
msgid ""
"Beta is the first level towards the stabilisation of what was before a fast "
"moving stream of changes. Specific projects may have slightly different "
"terminology for such releases (alpha, beta, etc) but all of these are "
"welcome on this risk level. These releases will almost certainly have passed "
"some sort of review and QA, but may still have unfinished parts. Breaking "
"changes are still relatively common here."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../explanation/how-snaps-work/channels-and-tracks.md:93
msgid "**edge**:  for users wanting to closely track development."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../explanation/how-snaps-work/channels-and-tracks.md:95
msgid ""
"Edge releases often include a moving stream of changes without QA or review "
"promises and are typically built automatically by a CI process from an "
"arbitrary source code snapshot. Often the CI will only publish after some "
"sort of automatic QA passed, and code reviews remain a good practice, but "
"these are project specific. Assume edge releases may break often."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../explanation/how-snaps-work/channels-and-tracks.md:97
msgid ""
"Snaps are installed from the stable risk-level by default. For example, the "
"following command installs VLC from its stable channel:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../explanation/how-snaps-work/channels-and-tracks.md:99
msgid "sudo snap install vlc\n"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../explanation/how-snaps-work/channels-and-tracks.md:103
msgid ""
"Use the `--channel` option to select a different risk-level. The following "
"command will install the latest beta snap of VLC:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../explanation/how-snaps-work/channels-and-tracks.md:105
msgid "sudo snap install --channel=beta vlc\n"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../explanation/how-snaps-work/channels-and-tracks.md:109
msgid ""
"If the beta snap is not available, a snap will be installed from the closest "
"channel with a more stable risk-level."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../explanation/how-snaps-work/channels-and-tracks.md:112
msgid ""
"ⓘ For brevity,  `--stable`, `--candidate`, `--beta` and `--edge` can be used "
"instead of `--channel=<risk-level>`"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../explanation/how-snaps-work/channels-and-tracks.md:114
msgid ""
"After installation, the risk-level being tracked can be changed with the use "
"of the `switch` command option:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../explanation/how-snaps-work/channels-and-tracks.md:116
msgid "sudo snap switch --channel=stable vlc\n"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../explanation/how-snaps-work/channels-and-tracks.md:120
msgid ""
"This option will not automatically refresh the snap to force the "
"installation of a new snap. To switch channels and update the snap with a "
"single command, add the `--channel` option to the *refresh* command:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../explanation/how-snaps-work/channels-and-tracks.md:123
msgid "sudo snap refresh --channel=stable vlc\n"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../explanation/how-snaps-work/channels-and-tracks.md:127
msgid ""
"To check which channel a snap is tracking, look for the *tracking* field in "
"the output from the `snap info` command:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../explanation/how-snaps-work/channels-and-tracks.md:129
msgid ""
"$ snap info vlc\n"
"[...]\n"
"snap-id:      RT9mcUhVsRYrDLG8qnvGiy26NKvv6Qkd\n"
"tracking:     edge\n"
"refresh-date: yesterday at 19:54 BST\n"
"[...]\n"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../explanation/how-snaps-work/channels-and-tracks.md:138
msgid ""
"Risk-levels may not match a project's internal conventions. Some projects "
"may use *alpha* instead of *edge*, for instance. However, a project's own "
"release nomenclature should be close enough to a snap's risk-levels to allow "
"you to judge the relative stability of the version you're installing."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../explanation/how-snaps-work/channels-and-tracks.md:141
msgid "Branches"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../explanation/how-snaps-work/channels-and-tracks.md:143
msgid ""
"A branch is an optional, fine-grained subdivision of a channel for a "
"published snap that allows for the creation of short-lived sequences of "
"snaps that can be pushed on demand by snap developers to help with fixes or "
"temporary experimentation."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../explanation/how-snaps-work/channels-and-tracks.md:145
msgid ""
"Branch names convey their purpose, such as `fix-for-bug123`, but the name "
"isn't exposed in the normal way, such as with the `snap info` command. "
"Instead, they can only be installed by someone who knows the branch name, "
"and this is usually only shared by the snap developer to test a specific fix "
"or release."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../explanation/how-snaps-work/channels-and-tracks.md:147
msgid ""
"After 30 days with no further updates, a branch will be closed "
"automatically. The replacement snap will then be chosen as it would be with "
"closed channels (see below). For example, *beta/fix-for-bug123* will fall "
"back to *beta* after the *fix-for-bug123* branch is closed."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../explanation/how-snaps-work/channels-and-tracks.md:149
msgid ""
"See Snapcraft's [Manage revisions and releases](https://"
"documentation.ubuntu.com/snapcraft/stable/how-to/publishing/manage-revisions-"
"and-releases/) for more details on how snap developers can use branches to "
"publish temporary snap releases."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../explanation/how-snaps-work/channels-and-tracks.md:151
msgid "Closing channels"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../explanation/how-snaps-work/channels-and-tracks.md:153
msgid ""
"A channel can be closed by a snap publisher when there is no longer a snap "
"that fits a channel's original purpose or specification."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../explanation/how-snaps-work/channels-and-tracks.md:155
msgid ""
"For example, when a specific risk-level channel is closed, the snap store "
"will select a snap from a more stable risk-level of the same track. If the "
"original channel is re-opened, snaps will once again be selected from the "
"original channel."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../explanation/how-snaps-work/channels-and-tracks.md:157
msgid ""
"This approach is commonly used for beta testing. If a snap is following a "
"*beta* channel that is then closed, the store will offer the snap from the "
"*candidate* channel. If the *candidate* channel is not available, the snap "
"from the *stable* channel will be selected. If the *beta* channel re-opens, "
"the snap will once again be selected from that channel."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../explanation/how-snaps-work/channels-and-tracks.md:159
msgid ""
"In some cases, when a specific revision is given, it may appear that a snap "
"is being installed from a less stable channel when a revision is not "
"available on the  more stable channel being tracked:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../explanation/how-snaps-work/channels-and-tracks.md:161
msgid ""
"$ snap install hello-world --revision=28\n"
"hello-world (edge) 6.3 from Canonical✓ installed\n"
"Channel latest/stable for hello-world is closed; temporarily forwarding to "
"edge.\n"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../explanation/how-snaps-work/channels-and-tracks.md:166
msgid ""
"This does not mean that the snap is now being tracked from a potentially "
"riskier channel (_edge_, in the above example), but that the requested "
"revision could only fetched from a riskier channel:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../explanation/how-snaps-work/channels-and-tracks.md:168
msgid ""
"$ snapcraft list-revisions hello-world\n"
"Rev.    Uploaded              Arches    Version    Channels\n"
"29      2019-04-17T16:43:58Z  all       6.4        latest/beta*,latest/"
"candidate*,latest/edge*,latest/stable*\n"
"28      2017-11-20T07:59:46Z  all       6.3        latest/edge\n"
"27      2016-07-12T16:37:23Z  all       6.3        latest/beta,latest/"
"candidate,latest/edge,latest/stable\n"
"26      2016-05-31T07:02:32Z  all       6.1        latest/edge,latest/"
"stable\n"
"24      2016-04-19T15:37:06Z  all       6          latest/beta,latest/"
"candidate,latest/edge,latest/stable\n"
msgstr ""
